  cites Foreword chapter Maybe
  cites Foreword chapter Result

 This is the implementation of the Network effect.

 fetch, post and resolve-dns were declared as operations of the
 Network effect in Foreword chapter Network and no handler for them
 existed anywhere, so the type checker vouched for the name and the
 build died at the end of emit with CDX2040. A name the compiler
 promises and cannot keep is worth less than no name. They are real
 here, and they are not in the foreword, for the reason that decides
 it: an implementation needs NetIO, NetIO is codex.os, and a foreword
 chapter may not depend on codex.os. A chapter that wants them cites
 this one.

 The declared rows were wrong as well as unimplemented. fetch was
 typed [Network.Read] -- but fetching sends a SYN and sends the
 request line, and net-io-connect says [Network.Read, Network.Write]
 because that is what it does. A read that writes to the wire is the
 laundering the effect system exists to refuse, and this quire is
 effect-exempt, so nothing would have caught it. All three operations
 declare both rows. The name says what it is for; the row says what
 it does.

 Scope is unaffected: lint-scoped-call keys on the callee's name, not
 on whether the name is a builtin, so fetch "https://host/x" is still
 checked against a Network.Read grant and still refused with CDX4002.

 We say:

Section: Network Configuration

 The addresses codex-vm's user-mode NAT hands out. The gateway
 answers ARP and forwards; 10.0.2.3 is its DNS.

  http-local-mac : List Integer = [82, 84, 0, 18, 52, 99]
  http-local-ip : List Integer = [10, 0, 2, 15]
  http-gateway-ip : List Integer = [10, 0, 2, 2]
  http-dns-ip : List Integer = [10, 0, 2, 3]
  http-local-port : Integer = 49232
  http-default-port : Integer = 80

Section: Character Codes

 Never write a character code down. Text is CCE inside and ASCII on
 the wire, and the two do not agree: in CCE the newline is 1 and the
 code 10 is the digit seven. Every code here is asked for.

  cce-colon : Integer = char-code (char-at ":" 0)
  cce-slash : Integer = char-code (char-at "/" 0)
  cce-dot : Integer = char-code (char-at "." 0)
  cce-zero : Integer = char-code (char-at "0" 0)
  cce-nine : Integer = char-code (char-at "9" 0)

  http-is-digit : Integer -> Boolean
  http-is-digit (c) = if c >= cce-zero then c <= cce-nine else False

  http-digit-value : Integer -> Integer
  http-digit-value (c) = c - cce-zero

Section: URL Parsing

 A URL is scheme://authority/path. The authority is host or host:port.
 The scheme is read, and it decides whether the request happens at all:
 https is refused, because this speaks HTTP and would otherwise fetch
 it in the clear. See The Refusal, below.

  url-authority-start : Text -> Integer
  url-authority-start (u) = url-scan-scheme u 0 (text-length u)

  url-scheme : Text -> Text
  url-scheme (u) =
   let s = url-authority-start u
   in if s < 3 then "" else substring u 0 (s - 3)

  url-scan-scheme : Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  url-scan-scheme (u) (i) (len) =
   if i + 2 >= len then 0
   else if char-code (char-at u i) == cce-colon then
     if char-code (char-at u (i + 1)) == cce-slash then
       if char-code (char-at u (i + 2)) == cce-slash then i + 3
       else url-scan-scheme u (i + 1) len
     else url-scan-scheme u (i + 1) len
   else url-scan-scheme u (i + 1) len

  url-authority-end : Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  url-authority-end (u) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then len
   else if char-code (char-at u i) == cce-slash then i
   else url-authority-end u (i + 1) len

  http-authority : Text -> Text
  http-authority (u) =
   let s = url-authority-start u
   in let e = url-authority-end u s (text-length u)
   in substring u s (e - s)

  url-host : Text -> Text
  url-host (u) =
   let a = http-authority u
   in let c = url-find-colon a 0 (text-length a)
   in if c < 0 then a else substring a 0 c

  url-find-colon : Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  url-find-colon (a) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then (-1)
   else if char-code (char-at a i) == cce-colon then i
   else url-find-colon a (i + 1) len

  url-port : Text -> Integer
  url-port (u) =
   let a = http-authority u
   in let c = url-find-colon a 0 (text-length a)
   in if c < 0 then http-default-port
   else let p = url-parse-int a (c + 1) (text-length a) 0 False
   in if p <= 0 then http-default-port else p

  url-parse-int : Text, Integer, Integer, Integer, Boolean -> Integer
  url-parse-int (s) (i) (len) (acc) (any) =
   if i >= len then (if any then acc else (-1))
   else let c = char-code (char-at s i)
   in if http-is-digit c then url-parse-int s (i + 1) len (acc * 10 + http-digit-value c) True
   else (if any then acc else (-1))

  url-path : Text -> Text
  url-path (u) =
   let s = url-authority-start u
   in let e = url-authority-end u s (text-length u)
   in if e >= text-length u then "/" else substring u e (text-length u - e)

Section: Host Addresses

 A host that is already a dotted quad is not looked up. Anything else
 goes to the resolver on the wire.

  http-is-dotted-quad : Text -> Boolean
  http-is-dotted-quad (h) = http-quad-scan h 0 (text-length h) 0 False

  http-quad-scan : Text, Integer, Integer, Integer, Boolean -> Boolean
  http-quad-scan (h) (i) (len) (dots) (any) =
   if i >= len then (if dots == 3 then any else False)
   else let c = char-code (char-at h i)
   in if c == cce-dot then http-quad-scan h (i + 1) len (dots + 1) False
   else if http-is-digit c then http-quad-scan h (i + 1) len dots True
   else False

  http-parse-quad : Text -> List Integer
  http-parse-quad (h) = http-quad-parts h 0 (text-length h) 0 False []

  http-quad-parts : Text, Integer, Integer, Integer, Boolean, List Integer -> List Integer
  http-quad-parts (h) (i) (len) (cur) (any) (acc) =
   if i >= len then (if any then list-push acc cur else acc)
   else let c = char-code (char-at h i)
   in if c == cce-dot then http-quad-parts h (i + 1) len 0 False (list-push acc cur)
   else http-quad-parts h (i + 1) len (cur * 10 + http-digit-value c) True acc

Section: DNS On The Wire

 An A-record query to the NAT's resolver over UDP. The destination MAC
 is the broadcast address because the ARP cache starts empty and the
 NAT answers for the gateway either way -- this is what NetworkStack's
 resolve-dst-mac already does for every TCP connection in the tree.

  http-dns-budget : Integer = 3000000
  http-dns-tx-id : Integer = 4919

  http-dns-query : Text -> [Network.Read, Network.Write] List Integer
  http-dns-query (host) =
   let frame = udp-build-frame mac-broadcast http-local-mac http-local-ip http-dns-ip 1024 dns-port (dns-build-a-query http-dns-tx-id host)
   in act
    sent <- net-driver-send-frame frame
    http-dns-await host 0
   end

  http-dns-await : Text, Integer -> [Network.Read] List Integer
  http-dns-await (host) (tries) =
   if tries > http-dns-budget then []
   else let hp = __heap-save
   in act
    frame <- net-driver-recv-frame
    if list-length frame == 0 then let restored = __heap-restore hp
      in http-dns-await host (tries + 1)
    else let addr = http-dns-extract frame
    in if list-length addr == 4 then addr
    else let restored2 = __heap-restore hp
    in http-dns-await host (tries + 1)
   end

 The reply is an ethernet frame carrying IP carrying UDP carrying the
 DNS answer. Anything that is not that is not our reply, and a frame
 that is not ours must cost nothing, which is what the heap-restore
 above is for.

  http-dns-extract : List Integer -> List Integer
  http-dns-extract (frame) =
   if eth-ethertype frame /= eth-type-ipv4 then []
   else let ip = eth-payload frame
   in if ip-proto ip /= ip-proto-udp then []
   else let dg = udp-parse (ip-payload ip)
   in let resp = dns-parse-response (dg.payload)
   in when dns-first-a-record resp
    is Just (a) -> a
    is None -> []

  http-host-address : Text -> [Network.Read, Network.Write] List Integer
  http-host-address (host) =
   if http-is-dotted-quad host then http-parse-quad host
   else http-dns-query host

Section: Response Framing

 HTTP/1.0 has two ways to end a body: the server names its length, or
 the server closes. Both are honoured. The length is the one that lets
 the read stop without waiting for a FIN, so it is checked first.

  http-headers-end : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  http-headers-end (bs) (i) (len) =
   if i + 3 >= len then (-1)
   else if list-at bs i == 13 then
     if list-at bs (i + 1) == 10 then
       if list-at bs (i + 2) == 13 then
         if list-at bs (i + 3) == 10 then i + 4
         else http-headers-end bs (i + 1) len
       else http-headers-end bs (i + 1) len
     else http-headers-end bs (i + 1) len
   else http-headers-end bs (i + 1) len

  http-body-complete : List Integer -> Boolean
  http-body-complete (bs) =
   let he = http-headers-end bs 0 (list-length bs)
   in if he < 0 then False
   else let cl = http-content-length bs he
   in if cl < 0 then False
   else list-length bs - he >= cl

Section: Receive

 A receive loop of our own rather than net-io-recv-raw, because that
 one returns only when a frame carried payload and so cannot see the
 FIN that ends a close-delimited body: it would spin out its whole
 budget after the last byte had already arrived. This one watches the
 connection state and stops on the close.

 The response is accumulated into ONE list, reserved up front and
 pushed into in place. The obvious loop -- acc & chunk, once per
 segment -- copies the whole accumulator every 1400 bytes, which is
 quadratic in the number of segments: a 1 MB body arrives in ~750
 segments and would allocate something like 400 MB to receive, none of
 it reclaimable before the call returns, on a heap with no collector.
 list-push writes in place while the list is under capacity, so the
 reservation makes the accumulation linear. The cap is the reservation,
 and a body past it stops the read rather than growing the list.

 The header offset and the declared length are found once and then
 carried, for the same reason: rescanning the accumulator every segment
 to answer "are we done yet" is the same quadratic in time.

  http-recv-budget : Integer = 3000000
  http-max-response : Integer = 1048576

  HttpRecv = record {
    transport : TcpTransportState,
    data : List Integer
  }

  http-append : List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  http-append (acc) (chunk) (i) (n) =
   if i >= n then acc
   else if list-length acc >= http-max-response then acc
   else http-append (list-push acc (list-at chunk i)) chunk (i + 1) n

  http-recv : TcpTransportState, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Network.Read, Network.Write] HttpRecv
  http-recv (ts) (acc) (head-end) (want) (tries) =
   if tries > http-recv-budget then HttpRecv { transport = ts, data = acc }
   else if http-have-all acc head-end want then HttpRecv { transport = ts, data = acc }
   else if list-length acc >= http-max-response then HttpRecv { transport = ts, data = acc }
   else let hp = __heap-save
   in act
    frame <- net-driver-recv-frame
    if list-length frame == 0 then let restored = __heap-restore hp
      in http-recv ts acc head-end want (tries + 1)
    else let r = net-process-frame (ts.session) frame
    in act
      ts1 <- flush-transport-outbox (TcpTransportState { session = r.session, recv-buf = ts.recv-buf, recv-base = ts.recv-base, recv-len = ts.recv-len, recv-cap = ts.recv-cap, messages-sent = ts.messages-sent, messages-received = ts.messages-received })
      let acc2 = http-append acc (r.data) 0 (list-length (r.data))
      in let he = if head-end < 0 then http-headers-end acc2 0 (list-length acc2) else head-end
      in let cl = if want < 0 then (if he < 0 then (-1) else http-content-length acc2 he) else want
      in when (ts1.session).conn.state
       is TcpEstablished -> http-recv ts1 acc2 he cl (tries + 1)
       is otherwise -> HttpRecv { transport = ts1, data = acc2 }
    end
   end

  http-have-all : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  http-have-all (acc) (head-end) (want) =
   if head-end < 0 then False
   else if want < 0 then False
   else list-length acc - head-end >= want

Section: The Network Scope Gate

 The runtime chokepoint for the network authority scope, the twin of
 Fat16's fat16-scope-admits for the filesystem path. A
 program declaring [Network.Write "api.example.com"] has that authority
 written into its process-table cell by the boot grant (or copied in at
 spawn, or installed by the verified loader). The compile time checker
 only sees literal URLs; a URL built at runtime from input is exactly
 what this gate is here to catch, so the check reads the destination
 authority off the runtime url and compares it to the grant with the
 same relation the type checker uses: a host matches whole, a port is
 compared only when the grant names one, and an empty grant admits every
 host (an unscoped program is unrestricted, which is what the empty
 authority already means).

 net-request-authority mirrors the type checker's url-authority: it
 appends the scheme's default port when the url leaves it implicit, so a
 grant written "host:80" and a fetch of "http://host/" agree on the port
 the way they do at compile time.

  auth-host : Text -> Text
  auth-host (a) =
   let c = url-find-colon a 0 (text-length a)
   in if c < 0 then a else substring a 0 c

  auth-port : Text -> Text
  auth-port (a) =
   let c = url-find-colon a 0 (text-length a)
   in if c < 0 then "" else substring a (c + 1) (text-length a - c - 1)

  net-authority-admits : Text, Text -> Boolean
  net-authority-admits (grant) (res) =
   if auth-host grant /= auth-host res then False
   else let gp = auth-port grant
   in let rp = auth-port res
   in if text-length gp == 0 then True
      else if text-length rp == 0 then True
      else gp == rp

  net-request-authority : Text -> Text
  net-request-authority (url) =
   let a = http-authority url
   in if url-find-colon a 0 (text-length a) >= 0 then a
      else a & ":" & (if url-scheme url == "https" then "443" else "80")

  net-scope-admits : Text -> Boolean
  net-scope-admits (url) =
   let grant = process-get-network-scope process-get-pid
   in if text-length grant == 0 then True
      else net-authority-admits grant (net-request-authority url)

Section: The Request

 Every way this can fail is a distinct Err, and none of them is an
 empty string. The first cut returned Text, so a host that would not
 resolve, a host that would not answer, and a server that returned an
 empty body were all "" and the caller could not tell them apart. A
 silently wrong answer is the thing this project least tolerates, and
 an error channel is the cheapest possible way not to give one.

  http-request : Text, Text, Text -> [Network.Read, Network.Write] Result Text Text
  http-request (method) (url) (body) =
   if net-scope-admits url == False then Err ("network: " & net-request-authority url & " is outside this program's grant")
   else let scheme = url-scheme url
   in if scheme == "https" then Err (https-refusal url)
   else let host = url-host url
   in let path = url-path url
   in let port = url-port url
   in act
    addr <- http-host-address host
    if list-length addr /= 4 then Err ("dns: cannot resolve " & host)
    else let sess = net-session-new http-local-mac http-local-ip http-gateway-ip http-local-port port addr 1000
    in let req = if method == "POST" then http-post-request host path body else http-get-request host path
    in let bytes = http-serialize-request req
    in act
      ts1 <- net-io-connect (transport-new sess)
      when (ts1.session).conn.state
       is TcpEstablished -> http-exchange ts1 bytes
       is otherwise -> Err ("connect: " & host & ":" & show port & " did not answer")
    end
   end

  http-exchange : TcpTransportState, List Integer -> [Network.Read, Network.Write] Result Text Text
  http-exchange (ts) (bytes) = act
    ts2 <- net-io-send-raw ts bytes
    r <- http-recv ts2 (__list-with-capacity http-max-response) (-1) (-1) 0
    ts3 <- net-io-close (r.transport)
    let resp = http-parse-response (r.data)
    in if resp.valid then Ok (resp.body) else Err "http: no response"
  end

Section: The Refusal

 An https URL is not fetched by THIS function. It would be fetched in
 the CLEAR -- the cleartext path speaks HTTP/1.0 and negotiates nothing
 -- and a caller that wrote https has said, in the only way the URL
 lets them, that the bytes must not cross the wire unprotected.
 Honouring the scheme as a parse and ignoring it as a promise is how a
 transport-security bug gets shipped, so the request is refused.

 What a refusal must not do is misdescribe itself, and this one did for
 as long as it said "TLS is not implemented here". TLS IS implemented
 here: `fetch-tls`, in the section below, runs a real TLS 1.3 handshake
 with Ed25519 X.509 authentication over this same transport. What
 `fetch` lacks is not the transport. It is the two things a caller has
 to supply and this chapter must never invent -- entropy (an ephemeral
 X25519 private key and a 32-byte handshake random) and a security
 policy (trust anchors, and the calendar time to check a certificate
 against). A one-argument `fetch` has nowhere to carry either, so it
 cannot authenticate anybody, and an anonymous connection under the
 https scheme is exactly the false promise this project does not ship.

 So the refusal is permanent by design rather than pending on somebody
 doing the work, and it names the function that does the job. A
 diagnostic that sends its reader looking for an unimplemented feature
 is worse than one that says nothing, because it looks like an answer.

  https-refusal : Text -> Text
  https-refusal (url) = "https: refusing to fetch " & url & " in the clear. fetch carries no trust anchors and no entropy, so it cannot authenticate a server; use fetch-tls, which takes both"

Section: HTTPS Over TLS 1.3

 The authenticated https path. This is what makes `https` mean https rather
 than a refusal: it runs a real TLS 1.3 handshake over the same TCP
 transport the cleartext path uses, then carries the HTTP request and
 response as application data under the negotiated keys. The TLS itself --
 the handshake, the record protection, the Ed25519 X.509 authentication,
 the man-in-the-middle defence -- is `Encode chapter TlsEndpoint`, proven end
 to end by `codex/test/apps/tls-fetch-loopback`.

 Two things enter from the caller and are never invented here, because a
 chapter that invents them invents a weak key or a lie: the client's
 ephemeral X25519 private key and 32-byte handshake random (entropy), and
 the trust anchors plus calendar time the server's certificate is checked
 against (the security policy). A caller with no anchors gets no
 authentication and this refuses to pretend otherwise.

 The bare one-argument `fetch` still refuses https below: it has nowhere to
 carry anchors or entropy, so it cannot make an authenticated connection,
 and an anonymous one under the https scheme would be exactly the false
 promise this project does not ship. Authenticated fetch is this function.

 The live exchange cannot run in the headless battery -- it starts no TLS
 server -- so in the battery this is compile-verified and rests on the
 loopback, the same standing as the cleartext live half.
 `build/https-interop-test.ps1` is the other half and it is the one that
 matters: three OpenSSL servers, an ECDSA P-256 chain and an RSA-2048
 chain that must both authenticate and deliver the served file byte for
 byte, and an unanchored self-signed leaf that must be refused. Until it
 existed this path had only ever been driven by code that agreed with it.

  https-port : Text -> Integer
  https-port (url) =
   let a = http-authority url
   in if url-find-colon a 0 (text-length a) >= 0 then url-port url else 443

  fetch-tls : Text, List Integer, List Integer, List X509Anchor, List Integer -> [Network.Read, Network.Write] Result Text Text
  fetch-tls (url) (client-priv) (random) (anchors) (cal-now) =
   if net-scope-admits url == False then Err ("network: " & net-request-authority url & " is outside this program's grant")
   else if url-scheme url /= "https" then Err "fetch-tls: url is not https"
   else fetch-tls-connect url (url-host url) (url-path url) (https-port url) client-priv random anchors cal-now

  fetch-tls-connect : Text, Text, Text, Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List X509Anchor, List Integer -> [Network.Read, Network.Write] Result Text Text
  fetch-tls-connect (url) (host) (path) (port) (client-priv) (random) (anchors) (cal-now) = act
    addr <- http-host-address host
    if list-length addr /= 4 then Err ("dns: cannot resolve " & host)
    else let sess = net-session-new http-local-mac http-local-ip http-gateway-ip http-local-port port addr 1000
    in act
      ts1 <- net-io-connect (transport-new sess)
      when (ts1.session).conn.state
       is TcpEstablished -> https-run ts1 host path client-priv random anchors cal-now
       is otherwise -> Err ("connect: " & host & ":" & show port & " did not answer")
    end
  end

 One TLS record framed off the TCP byte stream. A record is a five-byte
 header whose last two bytes are the length of what follows, so the whole
 record is 5 + that. Bytes are pulled until a full record is present; a
 stream that stops before one completes ends the read rather than blocking
 forever.

  HttpsRecv = record {
    hr-ts : TcpTransportState,
    hr-rec : List Integer,
    hr-rest : List Integer,
    hr-ok : Boolean
  }

  HttpsHs = record {
    hh-ts : TcpTransportState,
    hh-ep : TlsEp,
    hh-rest : List Integer
  }

  tls-record-ready : List Integer -> Boolean
  tls-record-ready (buf) =
   if list-length buf < 5 then False
   else list-length buf >= 5 + (list-at buf 3 * 256 + list-at buf 4)

  tls-split-record : TcpTransportState, List Integer -> HttpsRecv
  tls-split-record (ts) (buf) =
   let n = 5 + (list-at buf 3 * 256 + list-at buf 4)
   in HttpsRecv { hr-ts = ts, hr-rec = http-slice buf 0 n, hr-rest = http-slice buf n (list-length buf - n), hr-ok = True }

  http-slice : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  http-slice (bs) (off) (n) = http-slice-loop bs off n 0 []

  http-slice-loop : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  http-slice-loop (bs) (off) (n) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= n then acc
   else if off + i >= list-length bs then acc
   else http-slice-loop bs off n (i + 1) (list-push acc (list-at bs (off + i)))

 The bytes are pulled with a loop of this chapter's own rather than
 net-io-recv-raw, for the reason http-recv above gives about the cleartext
 path: net-io-recv-raw returns only when a frame carried payload, so it
 cannot tell a peer that has finished from a peer that is merely slow, and
 it spins out its whole fifty-million-iteration budget after the last byte
 has already arrived. The cleartext half was written that way from the
 start. This half was not, and nothing in the tree could show it: every
 loopback here keeps its connection open forever, so the spin never
 happened. The first foreign server this fetch ever met sent its response,
 sent a FIN, had the FIN acknowledged by the stack, and the client then sat
 for minutes with the entire body already in hand.

 An empty answer ends the read, and it means either the connection closed
 or the budget ran out. The caller does the same thing in both cases -- it
 stops -- so the two are not distinguished here; a field nobody reads is
 worth what no field is worth.

  HttpsPull = record {
    hp-ts : TcpTransportState,
    hp-data : List Integer
  }

  https-pull : TcpTransportState, Integer -> [Network.Read, Network.Write] HttpsPull
  https-pull (ts) (tries) =
   if tries > http-recv-budget then HttpsPull { hp-ts = ts, hp-data = [] }
   else let hp = __heap-save
   in act
    frame <- net-driver-recv-frame
    if list-length frame == 0 then let restored = __heap-restore hp
      in https-pull ts (tries + 1)
    else let r = net-process-frame (ts.session) frame
    in act
      ts1 <- flush-transport-outbox (TcpTransportState { session = r.session, recv-buf = ts.recv-buf, recv-base = ts.recv-base, recv-len = ts.recv-len, recv-cap = ts.recv-cap, messages-sent = ts.messages-sent, messages-received = ts.messages-received })
      if list-length (r.data) > 0 then HttpsPull { hp-ts = ts1, hp-data = r.data }
      else when (ts1.session).conn.state
       is TcpEstablished -> https-pull ts1 (tries + 1)
       is otherwise -> HttpsPull { hp-ts = ts1, hp-data = [] }
    end
   end

  tls-recv-record : TcpTransportState, List Integer, Integer -> [Network.Read, Network.Write] HttpsRecv
  tls-recv-record (ts) (buf) (guard) =
   if guard <= 0 then HttpsRecv { hr-ts = ts, hr-rec = [], hr-rest = buf, hr-ok = False }
   else if tls-record-ready buf then tls-split-record ts buf
   else act
    rr <- https-pull ts 0
    if list-length (rr.hp-data) == 0 then HttpsRecv { hr-ts = rr.hp-ts, hr-rec = [], hr-rest = buf, hr-ok = False }
    else tls-recv-record (rr.hp-ts) (buf & (rr.hp-data)) (guard - 1)
   end

  https-send-all : TcpTransportState, List (List Integer) -> [Network.Write] TcpTransportState
  https-send-all (ts) (recs) = https-send-loop ts recs 0

  https-send-loop : TcpTransportState, List (List Integer), Integer -> [Network.Write] TcpTransportState
  https-send-loop (ts) (recs) (i) =
   if i >= list-length recs then ts
   else act
    ts1 <- net-io-send-raw ts (list-at recs i)
    https-send-loop ts1 recs (i + 1)
   end

 Drive the handshake: send our ClientHello, then for every server record
 feed it to the endpoint and send whatever the endpoint answers, until the
 endpoint reports the handshake done or the stream stalls.

 The host out of the URL is what the certificate is checked against, in
 ASCII, because a certificate holds ASCII and `host` is CCE Text. This is
 the whole reason an https fetch can mean anything: the authority in the
 URL is the identity the caller asked for, so it is the identity the
 server has to prove. A certificate that is genuine but belongs to another
 host now fails the handshake instead of completing it.

  https-run : TcpTransportState, Text, Text, List Integer, List Integer, List X509Anchor, List Integer -> [Network.Read, Network.Write] Result Text Text
  https-run (ts) (host) (path) (client-priv) (random) (anchors) (cal-now) =
   let st0 = tls-ep-hello (tls-ep-client-new client-priv random anchors cal-now (http-to-ascii host))
   in act
    ts1 <- https-send-all ts (st0.tes-out)
    hs <- https-drive ts1 (st0.tes-ep) []
    if tls-ep-done (hs.hh-ep) == False then Err "https: handshake did not complete"
    else if tls-ep-authenticated (hs.hh-ep) == False then Err "https: server certificate not authenticated"
    else https-request hs host path
   end

  https-drive : TcpTransportState, TlsEp, List Integer -> [Network.Read, Network.Write] HttpsHs
  https-drive (ts) (ep) (buf) =
   if tls-ep-done ep then HttpsHs { hh-ts = ts, hh-ep = ep, hh-rest = buf }
   else act
    rr <- tls-recv-record ts buf 100000
    if rr.hr-ok == False then HttpsHs { hh-ts = rr.hr-ts, hh-ep = ep, hh-rest = rr.hr-rest }
    else let step = tls-ep-recv ep (rr.hr-rec)
    in act
     ts2 <- https-send-all (rr.hr-ts) (step.tes-out)
     https-drive ts2 (step.tes-ep) (rr.hr-rest)
    end
   end

 The request goes out as application data under the client key; the
 response records come back until the server closes, each decrypted and
 appended to the endpoint's application buffer, and that buffer is the HTTP
 response the ordinary parser then reads.

  https-request : HttpsHs, Text, Text -> [Network.Read, Network.Write] Result Text Text
  https-request (hs) (host) (path) =
   let send-step = tls-ep-send-app (hs.hh-ep) (http-serialize-request (http-get-request host path))
   in act
    ts1 <- https-send-all (hs.hh-ts) (send-step.tes-out)
    got <- https-recv-app ts1 (send-step.tes-ep) (hs.hh-rest) 0
    ts2 <- net-io-close (got.hh-ts)
    let parsed = http-parse-response (tls-ep-app-in (got.hh-ep))
    in if parsed.valid then Ok (parsed.body) else Err "https: no response"
   end

  https-recv-app : TcpTransportState, TlsEp, List Integer, Integer -> [Network.Read, Network.Write] HttpsHs
  https-recv-app (ts) (ep) (buf) (tries) =
   if tries > 3000000 then HttpsHs { hh-ts = ts, hh-ep = ep, hh-rest = buf }
   else act
    rr <- tls-recv-record ts buf 100000
    if rr.hr-ok == False then HttpsHs { hh-ts = rr.hr-ts, hh-ep = ep, hh-rest = rr.hr-rest }
    else https-recv-app (rr.hr-ts) ((tls-ep-recv ep (rr.hr-rec)).tes-ep) (rr.hr-rest) (tries + 1)
   end

Section: The Network Effect

  fetch : Text -> [Network.Read, Network.Write] Result Text Text
  fetch (url) = http-request "GET" url ""

  post : Text, Text -> [Network.Read, Network.Write] Result Text Text
  post (url) (body) = http-request "POST" url body

  resolve-dns : Text -> [Network.Read, Network.Write] Result Text Text
  resolve-dns (host) = act
    addr <- http-host-address host
    if list-length addr /= 4 then Err ("dns: cannot resolve " & host) else Ok (format-ip addr)
  end

